Weekly backlog burndown: in the ~10 hours before the account's weekly Codex (ChatGPT plan) usage reset, spend the remaining credits implementing backlog items across The Borg. The job fires twice because Codex's rolling 5-hour session limit caps what one run can burn: the first run typically dies at the session limit, and the second starts with a fresh session window and resumes the same plan. The fire times themselves live in `.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh` and are deliberately not repeated here — a schedule copied into a prompt drifts silently the moment the job is rescheduled.

This job runs headless via `codex exec` from `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po`. `.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh` grants its outer workspace-write sandbox three kinds of writable root, and both phases that commit depend on all of them: the workspace root, every repository's `.git` directory (Codex's workspace-write sandbox excludes `.git/` from a writable root, so without this nothing can be committed), and `$CODEX_HOME` (without which a nested `codex exec` child cannot start). That outer sandbox is the sole enforcement boundary for the whole process tree: spawned commands inherit it, and a child must not try to initialize a second macOS Seatbelt sandbox inside it. It is EXPECTED to die mid-run when credits run out — every phase therefore leaves durable state (commits, checked boxes) before moving on, and the plan email goes out before implementation starts.

The phases below are named, not numbered, so the paired `/backlog-burndown` command can reference them without breaking when this file is edited.

GATE — decide the run mode: FRESH, RESUME, or stop.
Determine the current ISO week with `date +%G-W%V` and read `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown.json` (if present).
- State file missing, or its `week` field is not the current ISO week → **FRESH**: run every phase below.
- `week` equals the current ISO week and the plan file (`${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown-plan.md`) still has unchecked actionable items → **RESUME**: skip PRIORITIZE, CONSOLIDATE, NOTIFY PLAN, and RECORD STATE; go straight to BURN at the first unchecked item. A prior run this week already planned, emailed, and recorded state — this run only continues the burn.
- `week` equals the current ISO week and the plan has no unchecked actionable items (or the plan file is missing) → stop and output nothing; the week's plan is spent.

WINDOW — abort if fired outside the pre-reset window.
Two facts drive this phase, and neither is a schedule:
- **Weekly usage reset: Saturday 7:09 AM local.** An account fact, read from `/status` in Codex. This is the one timing value that belongs in this file.
- **Rolling session limit: 5 hours.** A vendor fact, re-verified monthly by the assumptions audit (Assumption H).

Derive the valid window from those two — do not pin fire times here, and do not read them from the scheduler at run time either; the bounds below hold regardless of when launchd actually fires:
- earliest valid start = reset − (2 x session window) − 20min slack
- latest valid start  = reset − 30min

With today's values that is **Friday 20:49 through Saturday 06:39** (derived, not authoritative — recompute from the two facts above rather than trusting this line). The window spans midnight. If the current local time is outside it, stop and output nothing: launchd fires missed events on wake, so a sleeping Mac can delay a run past the reset, and running after the reset would burn the fresh week's budget, which defeats the purpose.

Two session windows is the sizing rationale — the job is built to fill roughly two consecutive session windows before the reset, which is also why it fires twice. If the account's reset time changes, update the reset fact above and re-derive the schedule in `.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh` from it; the assumptions audit verifies the two stay consistent.

PRIORITIZE — reorder every backlog in place.
Enumerate the backlogs by scanning the filesystem, never from memory or from a previous run's plan: `${BORG_ROOT}/BACKLOG.md` (workspace) and every `${BORG_ROOT}/repos/*/BACKLOG.md` (`repos/` is git-ignored, so its children never appear in workspace git status). At the time of writing that resolves to exactly two files — the workspace backlog and `repos/waiq/BACKLOG.md` — but the scan is authoritative and repos come and go; if it finds a different set, work the set it found and say so. Record the enumerated paths and each file's open-item count before reordering anything; CONSOLIDATE must account for every one of them. Reorder each file's open items by priority, preserving that file's own format and conventions (the workspace file is a flat checkbox list — reorder it highest-priority-first; a repo file with priority tiers keeps its tiers, moving items between tiers only when clearly warranted). Priority rubric: user pain in active use > bugs > security/correctness > features > nice-to-haves; weigh what unblocks other items. Do not rewrite item content. Commit each reordered file in its own repo (workspace file in the theborg repo, repo files in their repos).

CONSOLIDATE — build the run plan.
Write `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown-plan.md` (overwriting any previous run's plan): a single prioritized union of all open items across every backlog PRIORITIZE enumerated, one checkbox line per item with its source file, owning agent/repo, and dispatch directory (per BURN). Items a headless run cannot do get a `needs-user:` marker with a one-line reason instead of a checkbox — anything requiring a physical device, user-only verification, credentials or purchases, app-store/publishing actions, or a destructive/irreversible operation. Order the actionable rest by the same priority rubric.

The union is ranked strictly across sources, not grouped by source. Rank every item against every other item regardless of which backlog it came from: a workspace P-equivalent bug outranks a repo nice-to-have and vice versa. Per-file order from PRIORITIZE is an input, not a constraint — do not concatenate the files, and do not let one backlog's items monopolize the top merely because that file is longer or uses explicit tiers while another does not. A run of consecutive same-source items is fine when the rubric earns it; a plan whose order is explainable purely by source file is a merge failure, so re-rank before writing.

Open the plan with a coverage line for each enumerated backlog — `<path>: <open items found> found, <actionable> actionable, <needs-user> needs-user` — and the totals. Every enumerated backlog gets a row even when it contributed zero items, which is what makes a missed or empty backlog visible instead of silent. If a row's found count does not equal its actionable + needs-user counts, the merge dropped items: fix it before writing.

NOTIFY PLAN — email the plan before burning.
Pipe the plan into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg backlog burndown — {YYYY-MM-DD} — plan: {N} item(s), {K} needs-user"` (reads the message from stdin). Body header: same as the subject. This email is sent BEFORE implementation because the run is expected to die when credits run out; it is the user's record of what the run set out to do.

RECORD STATE — mark the week done before burning.
Write `${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/.claude/scheduled/state/c4po-backlog-burndown.json` with `{"week":"YYYY-Www","date":"YYYY-MM-DD","ts":"<ISO 8601 timestamp>","planned":<N>,"needs_user":<K>}`. `mkdir -p` the state directory first if needed. State is recorded now, not at the end, because the end may never arrive.

BURN — implement items until credits run out.
Loop over the plan top-down, dispatching each item to a FRESH headless child session — one `codex exec` per item. This keeps any item from paying for the context accumulated by the ones before it (context rot and token cost both grow with session length), and it loads the correct AGENTS.md chain for the item's owner. This session is the orchestrator only: it spawns children, verifies evidence, and updates trackers — it does not implement items itself.
- Dispatch directory: workspace item owned by an agent → `${BORG_ROOT}/<agent>`; owned by the workspace itself → `${BORG_ROOT}`; repo item → that repo's root under `${BORG_ROOT}/repos/`.
- Spawn: immediately before starting each child, record `git -C "<dispatch repo>" rev-parse HEAD` for that item. Then run `codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox "<child prompt>"` from the dispatch directory.
  - The flag name is intentionally alarming. It is safe here only because the child is already inside the outer `codex exec --sandbox workspace-write` process launched by `.bin/run-scheduled-task.sh`; macOS propagates that enforced boundary to spawned commands. OpenAI documents this flag specifically for processes running inside an externally hardened environment. Never use this child command from an unsandboxed shell or copy it into a standalone runner.
  - Do not pass `--sandbox`, `sandbox_workspace_write.*`, or `--add-dir` to a nested child. The 2026-08-07 run proved that initializing a second macOS Seatbelt sandbox fails before every child command with `sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted`. The child inherits the outer sandbox's workspace, repository `.git`, `$CODEX_HOME`, and network grants instead.
  - The consequence is deliberate and explicit: child sessions are not structurally narrowed to one repository. The outer sandbox allows the whole Borg workspace because the parent must prioritize and update trackers across it. Repository scope is therefore enforced by the self-contained child prompt, the recorded-SHA range, commit reconciliation, and the parent's inspection—not by a second sandbox.
  - Items that edit a slash command under any `.claude/commands/` need no child-specific `$CODEX_HOME/skills` grant. The outer runner already grants `$CODEX_HOME` to this task, so `.bin/sync-codex-prompts.sh` can regenerate the bridged skill.
  Pass no `-m` or effort overrides — model and reasoning effort come from `~/.codex/config.toml` defaults. No MCP flags are needed: codex loads MCP servers only from `~/.codex/config.toml`.
- The child prompt must be self-contained: the item's full text, its source BACKLOG.md path, and the standing rules — implement in the owning repo following its AGENTS.md, rules, and ADR conventions; verify as far as headless allows (run the repo's tests/build if it has them; never claim verification the item reserves for a device or the user); commit with a clear message; never push to any remote; never write `cerebruh/` wiki content (read-only for all agents per the workspace AGENTS.md — codex does not enforce Claude's deny rules, so state this rule in every child prompt); do NOT edit any BACKLOG.md or the plan file (the parent owns trackers); if the item proves infeasible headless, commit nothing and print `INFEASIBLE: <reason>`; otherwise print every commit SHA it created as its final line in the form `CREATED: <sha> [<sha> ...]`.
- Verify by evidence and reconciliation. Inspect only commits in `<recorded SHA>..HEAD` in the dispatch repo, then reconcile that range with the SHA(s) in the child's final output and inspect the reconciled commits' contents. The range bounds the candidates but does not prove authorship: a concurrent session can commit inside the window, as happened repeatedly on 2026-08-02. Reconciliation is what exposes that ambiguity. If the range and child output disagree, trust the range as the repository record, report the discrepancy, and do not guess which commits belong to the child.
- Run `git status` in the dispatch repo and report every uncommitted change. Leave all of them exactly as found. Never stage, commit, revert, stash, or check out an uncommitted change during verification, even when it appears to complete the item; on 2026-08-02 concurrent sessions wrote and committed in the same repositories during BURN, so ownership cannot be inferred from timing or relevance.
- Update trackers in the parent: check the item off (or move it to a Done section, per that file's convention) in its source BACKLOG.md and in the plan file; commit the tracker updates. If the child reported infeasible or left no commit, mark the item `skipped: <reason>` in the plan file instead and move on.
Continue until no actionable items remain — or the run dies with the credits, which is the expected exit; the commits and checked boxes are the durable record.

WRAP — only reached if the plan empties before the credits do.
Pipe a summary (items completed / skipped / needs-user, with commit hashes) into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg backlog burndown — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {C} done, {S} skipped"`. Body header: same as the subject.
